export const GITHUB_PROJECT_ENTRIES = [{"path":"/projects/README.md","type":"file","markdown":"# Public GitHub projects\n\nPublic repositories owned by [lfb0801](https://github.com/lfb0801?tab=repositories), refreshed hourly.\n\nOpen a project for its README and additional notes. Use **Add project notes** to propose more context through an issue and pull request.\n\n- [claude-remarc-sync](/projects/github/claude-remarc-sync.md)\n- [claude-skill-args](/projects/github/claude-skill-args.md)\n- [http-cats](/projects/github/http-cats.md)\n- [JavaPracticeChallenge](/projects/github/JavaPracticeChallenge.md)\n- [pickleball-hussel-systeem](/projects/github/pickleball-hussel-systeem.md)\n"},{"path":"/projects/github","type":"directory"},{"path":"/projects/github/claude-remarc-sync.md","type":"file","markdown":"# claude-remarc-sync\n\n[View on GitHub](https://github.com/lfb0801/claude-remarc-sync)\n\nA Claude Code mod that links a session to a Remarc session and works your comments as they arrive\n\n## README\n\n# claude-remarc-sync\n\nA Claude Code mod that links a session to a [Remarc](https://remarc.app) session and works your comments as they arrive. Press one button, comment in Remarc, and Claude claims, implements, verifies and resolves each comment without you prompting it. A pane keeps a running report of every change.\n\n```\nRemarc sync · Inbox · 4 resolved · 1 working · 0 queued   [ Report ]  [ Stop sync ]\n```\n\n## The idea\n\nRemarc collects feedback as you look at your work: text selections, screenshots, web elements, spoken crit notes. Normally you then switch to Claude and ask it to go through them.\n\nWith remarc-sync, the session keeps watching the Remarc session instead:\n\n| | Without remarc-sync | With remarc-sync |\n|---|---|---|\n| New comment | Sits in Remarc until you ask Claude | Picked up within 10 seconds |\n| Lifecycle | You remind Claude to claim and resolve | Claimed with `inProgress` first, resolved with a summary after it is verified |\n| Several comments | Worked one at a time | Independent ones go to parallel subagents or forks |\n| What happened | Scroll back through the transcript | One report pane: request, change, files, commit, verification, status |\n\n## Install\n\n```\n/plugin install remarc-sync --marketplace lfb0801/claude-remarc-sync\n```\n\nAnswer `y` to add the marketplace, then choose a scope.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Run this in a terminal session. The desktop Code tab does not offer `/plugin`, but a user-scope install also loads there.\n\nIt needs the Remarc plugin for Claude Code, which provides the `plugin:remarc:remarc` MCP server:\n\n```\n/plugin install remarc --marketplace metedata/remarc-agent-plugins\n```\n\n### Permissions\n\nThe mod calls three Remarc tools on its own: `remarc_list_sessions`, `remarc_list_comments` and `remarc_create_session`. Under auto mode the classifier has no conversation to judge a plugin's own call by and may refuse it, so allow them in `~/.claude/settings.json`:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"permissions\": {\n    \"allow\": [\n      \"mcp__plugin_remarc_remarc__remarc_list_sessions\",\n      \"mcp__plugin_remarc_remarc__remarc_list_comments\",\n      \"mcp__plugin_remarc_remarc__remarc_create_session\"\n    ]\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe two list tools only read; the third only creates a Remarc session. Claude's own Remarc calls (claiming and resolving comments) go through your normal permissions.\n\n## Use it\n\n1. Press **Start Remarc sync** in the band above the prompt.\n2. Comment in Remarc. Each new comment reaches Claude as a prompt within about 10 seconds, once no turn is running.\n3. Open **Report** to see what was done. It also opens by itself once three comments are worked, or as soon as a subagent or fork did one.\n\nWith the FantaMentos banner loaded, the sync is one of its tiles instead of a band of its own: `📝 Remarc` with the session and its counts (`🔧 1 ⏳ 2 ✅ 4`, `⚠` when a poll failed). Press it to open the sync's mode, which holds Start, Report and Stop sync. The mod publishes the tile as its `tile` state.\n\nThe session it links to:\n\n- The Remarc session already linked to this Claude Code session, if it is still active (remembered by the mod, or Remarc's own marker for the session).\n- Otherwise a new one, named after the project folder and the session id, created with `remarc_create_session` and your real session id.\n- Or one you name: `/remarc-sync on Inbox`, or ask Claude to link the sync to it.\n\n### Turn it off\n\nPress **Stop sync** in the band, type `/remarc-sync off`, or ask Claude to stop the Remarc sync. It stays off for that session across restarts; **Resume Remarc sync** links the same Remarc session again. The Remarc session itself stays: close it in the Remarc app.\n\n| Command | Does |\n|---|---|\n| `/remarc-sync on [session]` | Start, or relink to a named session |\n| `/remarc-sync off` | Stop polling |\n| `/remarc-sync report` | Open the report pane |\n| `/remarc-sync status` | Say what it is linked to and how many comments are resolved |\n\n## How it works\n\n- **Polling:** every 10 seconds the mod reads the linked session with `remarc_list_comments`. Comments that are `open` or `handedOff` and have not been handed to Claude yet go out in one prompt, through `$.prompt.submit`, only while no turn runs. Each comment goes out once; one you reopen after it was resolved goes out again.\n- **The prompt** tells Claude to read each comment with `remarc_get_comment`, claim it with `remarc_set_status` and `expected_status` (so two agents never work the same one), implement it under the project's own instructions, verify it, resolve it with a summary, and record it with the mod's `report_change` tool.\n- **The report** merges Remarc's statuses (from polling) with what Claude reported (files, commit, verification, who did it).\n- **Remarc app refresh:** when a status changes, the mod posts Remarc's `com.metepolat.Remarc.reload` notification through `osascript`, so the Remarc window shows it at once. Remarc's own server posts it with `swift -e`, which fails silently where the Swift toolchain and SDK versions differ.\n\nTools the mod gives Claude:\n\n| Tool | For |\n|---|---|\n| `mcp__remarc-sync__report_change` | Recording what was done per comment |\n| `mcp__remarc-sync__sync_control` | Turning the sync on or off, relinking, opening the report |\n\n## Requirements\n\n- Verified on Claude Code 2.1.304, with the Remarc plugin 0.13.3, on macOS.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Function hooks (`modules` in `hooks/hooks.json`) are early access and may change. Older CLIs ignore the module, or reject `hooks.json` outright.\n\n## Limitations\n\n- Comments are read from the text `remarc_list_comments` returns, which may change between Remarc releases.\n- A comment that arrives during a turn waits until that turn ends.\n- Comments go to the session that is active in the Remarc app. Switch to the linked session there, or comments land elsewhere.\n- Remarc's MCP tools cannot close a session, so stopping the sync leaves it in Remarc.\n\n## Development\n\n```\nclaude --plugin-dir .\nclaude plugin validate .\nclaude plugin test .\ntsc -p .\n```\n\n`tsc` needs `.claude-plugin/types/`, which Claude Code generates when it loads the mod. It is gitignored.\n\n\n[Add project notes](https://github.com/lfb0801/lfb0801/issues/new?template=project-notes.yml&title=Project+notes%3A+claude-remarc-sync&project=claude-remarc-sync)\n\n[All projects](/projects/README.md)\n"},{"path":"/projects/github/claude-skill-args.md","type":"file","markdown":"# claude-skill-args\n\n[View on GitHub](https://github.com/lfb0801/claude-skill-args)\n\nEnumerated !value arguments for user-invoked Claude Code skills, with autocomplete, clickable cards and prompts for missing values\n\n## README\n\n# claude-skill-args\n\nA Claude Code mod that lets any skill you write offer a fixed set of choices as `!value` arguments. Users get autocomplete and clickable cards, and the skill gets clean, predictable arguments.\n\n```\n/demo !friendly the weather\n```\n\n## The idea\n\nA skill normally gets one blob of free text and has to guess what the user meant:\n\n```\n/review take a detailed look at the onboarding redesign\n```\n\nWith skill-args, you declare the choices once in your skill's frontmatter. Users pick them with `!value`, and your skill reads each one as its own argument:\n\n```\n/review !thorough the onboarding redesign\n```\n\n| | Without skill-args | With skill-args |\n|---|---|---|\n| User | Has to remember what the skill understands | Picks from autocomplete or cards |\n| Missing input | The skill guesses or asks halfway through | Asked before the skill starts |\n| Choices | Free-form and easy to misspell | Picked from a declared list |\n| Skill body | Parses `$ARGUMENTS` itself | Reads `$depth` and `$target` |\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Your skill body doesn't change. skill-args only rewrites what the user typed into the standard positional arguments that Claude Code already supports, so `$name`, `$0` and `$ARGUMENTS[0]` work as usual.\n\n## Install\n\n```\n/plugin install skill-args --marketplace lfb0801/claude-skill-args\n```\n\nAnswer `y` to add the marketplace, then choose a scope.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Run this in a terminal session. The desktop Code tab does not offer `/plugin`, but a user-scope install also loads there.\n\n## Try the demo\n\n[examples/demo/SKILL.md](https://github.com/lfb0801/claude-skill-args/blob/master/examples/demo/SKILL.md) declares one choice (`tone`) and one free-text argument (`topic`), and echoes what it receives. It isn't installed as a command. To try it, copy it into your skills:\n\n```bash\nmkdir -p ~/.claude/skills/demo && cp examples/demo/SKILL.md ~/.claude/skills/demo/\n```\n\nThen type `/demo !friendly the weather`. Claude replies with:\n\n```\ntone=friendly topic=the weather\nfriendly | the weather\nfriendly \"the weather\"\n```\n\nType `/demo the weather` without a tone and you are asked which tone you want before the skill runs.\n\n## Use it in your own skill\n\nSet `disable-model-invocation: true`, declare choices under `metadata.args`, and use `arguments` to set their positional order. Each example below is a separate skill.\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> skill-args runs when *you* type a slash command. It never sees a skill that Claude invokes on its own, so the skill must set `disable-model-invocation: true`.\n\n### 1. Required choices: choose the review depth\n\nUse a required choice when the skill needs a decision before it can start.\n\n```yaml\n---\nname: review\ndescription: Review a piece of work\ndisable-model-invocation: true\narguments: [depth, target]\nmetadata:\n  args:\n    depth:\n      question: How detailed should the review be?\n      options:\n        brief: 🌱 Quick overview of key points\n        thorough: 🔎 Detailed review of each aspect\n    target:\n      question: What should be reviewed?\n---\n\nReview $target. Depth: $depth.\n```\n\nType `/review !thorough the redesign` to pass `thorough` as `$depth` and `the redesign` as `$target`. Both choices are suggested while typing. For example, terminal autocomplete filters by prefix:\n\n```\n> /review !t\n  !thorough    Detailed review of each aspect\n```\n\nSubmit `/review the redesign` without a depth and the skill waits for your choice. The terminal asks through Claude's question dialog:\n\n```\n ☐ depth\n\n How detailed should the review be?\n\n ❯ 1. brief — Quick overview of key points\n   2. thorough — Detailed review of each aspect\n   3. Type something.\n\n Enter to select · Esc to cancel\n```\n\nThe desktop shows cards in the banner above the prompt:\n\n```\n /review · How detailed should the review be?                    Cancel\n ╭────────────────────────────────╮  ╭────────────────────────────────╮\n │ 🌱 brief                       │  │ 🔎 thorough                    │\n │ Quick overview of key points   │  │ Detailed review of each aspect │\n ╰────────────────────────────────╯  ╰────────────────────────────────╯\n```\n\nAn invalid answer is asked again. In the terminal, Esc cancels and puts your text back in the prompt.\n\n### 2. Optional choices: add a focus when useful\n\nUse `optional: true` for a choice the skill can work without. Here, a document check can cover the whole document or focus on one area.\n\n```yaml\n---\nname: check\ndescription: Check a document\ndisable-model-invocation: true\narguments: [focus, document]\nmetadata:\n  args:\n    focus:\n      optional: true\n      options:\n        scope: 🎯 Review what is in and what is out\n        risks: 🚨 Identify potential issues\n    document:\n      question: Which document should be checked?\n---\n\nCheck $document. Focus on $focus if supplied; otherwise check the whole document.\n```\n\n| Typed | `$focus` / `$0` | `$document` / `$1` |\n|---|---|---|\n| `/check !scope the proposal` | `scope` | `the proposal` |\n| `/check the proposal` | *(empty)* | `the proposal` |\n\nThe declared choices can appear in autocomplete and typing cards. On submit, a missing optional choice never triggers a question or a required-choice banner. `/check the proposal` runs immediately, with an empty string in the first position: `$ARGUMENTS` is `\"\" \"the proposal\"`.\n\n### 3. Open choices: accept a custom audience\n\nUse `open: true` when common choices help, but users should also be able to name their own value.\n\n```yaml\n---\nname: explain\ndescription: Explain a topic for an audience\ndisable-model-invocation: true\narguments: [audience, topic]\nmetadata:\n  args:\n    audience:\n      open: true\n      question: Who is the explanation for?\n      options:\n        beginner: 🌱 Start with the basics\n        developer: 💻 Include technical details\n    topic:\n      question: What should be explained?\n---\n\nExplain $topic for $audience.\n```\n\n| Typed | `$audience` / `$0` | `$topic` / `$1` |\n|---|---|---|\n| `/explain !beginner caching` | `beginner` | `caching` |\n| `/explain !designer caching` | `designer` | `caching` |\n\n`beginner` and `developer` are suggested choices. The custom `!designer` value is accepted directly; it does not need to be declared. Type custom values in the command as `!value`: the question dialog and cards offer the declared choices.\n\n`open: true` does not make an argument optional. Submit `/explain caching` and you are still asked for an audience before the skill runs. Combine `open: true` with `optional: true` only when a custom value may also be omitted.\n\n### Argument reference\n\n| Key under `metadata.args.<name>` | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `options` | The declared values, each with a description. A leading emoji is shown on desktop cards. |\n| `question` | What to ask when a required value is missing. For choices, defaults to `Which <name>?`. |\n| `optional: true` | Don't ask for it on submit. A missing value reaches the skill as an empty string. Declared choices can still be suggested while typing. |\n| `open: true` | Also accept `!values` that aren't in the declared list. |\n| *(no `options`)* | The free-text argument: every plain word the user typed. At most one. |\n\nRules:\n\n- `arguments:` sets the positional order and enables `$name`. Leave it out and the order of `metadata.args` is used; then use `$0`, `$1`, and so on.\n- Every argument is required unless it sets `optional: true`.\n- The skill command and `!values` can be written anywhere in the prompt, in any order. For example, `Please /review the redesign` shows the argument cards and asks for the missing required depth before running. Every plain word, including words before the command, belongs to the free-text argument.\n- An unknown `!value` fills the first empty open argument. If there is none, it counts as an invalid value for the first empty required strict argument, which is then asked again. Otherwise it stays in the free text.\n- Prefer single-codepoint emoji in descriptions.\n- Skills without `metadata.args` behave exactly as before.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> `$ARGUMENTS` is the whole rewritten string, not what the user typed. For example, `/review !thorough the redesign` becomes `thorough \"the redesign\"`. Use `$name` or `$N` for single values. Quoted text in the input is preserved as part of the free-text argument.\n\n## How it works\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[\"You type a slash command\"] --> B{\"Does the skill declare metadata.args?\"}\n    B -- no --> Z[\"Runs untouched\"]\n    B -- yes --> C[\"Match each !value to an argument.<br/>Plain words become the free text.\"]\n    C --> D{\"Anything required missing?\"}\n    D -- \"a choice\" --> E[\"Ask for it:<br/>question dialog in the terminal,<br/>cards on the desktop\"]\n    E --> D\n    D -- \"the free text\" --> F[\"Put your text back in the prompt box\"]\n    D -- \"nothing\" --> G[\"Rewrite to positional arguments.<br/>Use an empty string for omitted optional values.\"]\n    G --> H[\"Claude Code splits them shell-style\"]\n    H --> I[\"The skill reads $name, $N and $ARGUMENTS[N]\"]\n```\n\n1. **While you type**, it reads the skill's `metadata.args` and suggests declared `!values` for arguments that have not been filled.\n2. **When you submit**, it intercepts the command, matches every `!value`, and asks for anything required that's missing.\n3. **Before the skill runs**, it rewrites your input to one quoted value per declared argument, with `\"\"` for an empty optional one, so every position stays in place.\n4. **Claude Code** then fills `$name`, `$N` and `$ARGUMENTS[N]` from that string, as it does for any skill.\n\nCommands at the beginning use Claude Code's normal command hook. A skill mentioned later in the prompt is checked before submission and dispatched as a command after all required arguments are supplied; the unchecked prompt is not sent to Claude.\n\n| | Terminal | Desktop app (Code tab) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Suggestions while typing | Native autocomplete rows for `!values`, after the command and after each completed value. A compact clickable list appears above the prompt when the native menu has nothing to show. | No native autocomplete exists there. A band of clickable cards follows what you type, including Tab completion. |\n| Missing required choice on submit | Asked through Claude's own question dialog. | Picked from cards in the banner above the prompt. |\n| Missing optional choice on submit | Runs without asking for it. | Runs without asking for it. |\n| Missing required free text on submit | Your text is put back in the prompt box. | Your text is put back in the prompt box. |\n\n## Where skill files are found\n\n- Namespaced `plugin:name`: through the plugin's own root, or its `installPath` in `~/.claude/plugins/installed_plugins.json`.\n- Plain names: through Claude's command list, then `.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`, then `~/.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`.\n\nIf the hook fails before it has read a skill's definition, the command runs untouched. If it fails after that, it refuses to run the command.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- Verified on Claude Code 2.1.293.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Function hooks (`modules` in `hooks/hooks.json`) are early access and may change. Older CLIs ignore the module, or reject `hooks.json` outright.\n\n## Limitations\n\n- The desktop app never calls the autocomplete hook, so the desktop uses cards instead.\n- A band cannot take keyboard focus without ctrl+x tab, so keyboard selection goes through native autocomplete or the question dialog.\n- Plugin skills are located through `~/.claude/plugins/installed_plugins.json`, an internal file whose format may change.\n- On the desktop the prompt is read once every 500ms, which is how Tab completion is noticed.\n\n> [!CAUTION]\n> Install only one copy of this feature. A plugin that bundles its own copy, installed alongside this mod, would handle the same skill twice.\n\n## Development\n\n```\nclaude --plugin-dir .\nclaude plugin validate .\nclaude plugin test .\ntsc -p .\n```\n\n`tsc` needs `.claude-plugin/types/`, which Claude Code generates when it loads the mod. It is gitignored.\n\n\n[Add project notes](https://github.com/lfb0801/lfb0801/issues/new?template=project-notes.yml&title=Project+notes%3A+claude-skill-args&project=claude-skill-args)\n\n[All projects](/projects/README.md)\n"},{"path":"/projects/github/http-cats.md","type":"file","markdown":"# http-cats\n\n[View on GitHub](https://github.com/lfb0801/http-cats)\n\nchrome extension that allows the user to look up their error code with http.cats\n\n## README\n\nThis repository has no README yet.\n\n[Add project notes](https://github.com/lfb0801/lfb0801/issues/new?template=project-notes.yml&title=Project+notes%3A+http-cats&project=http-cats)\n\n[All projects](/projects/README.md)\n"},{"path":"/projects/github/JavaPracticeChallenge.md","type":"file","markdown":"# JavaPracticeChallenge\n\n[View on GitHub](https://github.com/lfb0801/JavaPracticeChallenge)\n\nInside of this repository we can find the programming challenge I came up with to challenge Software Engineers on their Development Ability.\n\n## README\n\n# Java Practice Challenge\n\n## Overview\nThis repository contains a coding challenge designed to evaluate your development skills. As you will be joining a LARGE project, we focus on code quality, architectural decisions, and how your code supports long-term maintainability and team growth.\n\n## Getting Started\n1. **Understand the Requirements**: Read [Challenge.pdf](https://github.com/lfb0801/JavaPracticeChallenge/blob/master/Challenge.pdf).\n2. **Project Setup**: Create a directory in the root named after yourself (e.g., `/FirstNameLastName`).\n3. **Submit Your Solution**:\n   - Place your solution inside your personal directory.\n   - Use only the provided resources; do not use existing code found in the project.\n   - If you deviate from requirements, document your reasoning (e.g., in code comments or a separate Markdown file).\n\n## Expectations\nWe value high-quality code over a 100% complete but messy solution. Given the time limit, we expect intentional trade-offs.\n\n### Key Evaluation Areas\n- **Maintainability & Mentorship**: Is the code clear enough for a junior developer to understand and safely modify? We value code that sets a high standard for others.\n- **Intentionality**: Avoid \"copy-paste\" development. We look for a deep understanding of the tools and patterns you use, and why they are appropriate for this specific context.\n- **Extensibility**: How easily can features be added or behavior changed in a large-scale codebase?\n- **Architecture**: Clear separation of concerns, responsibility, and ownership.\n- **Consistency**: Maintain a consistent coding style throughout.\n- **Tech Stack**: Use the latest Java and Spring Boot with Gradle or Maven. A functional programming style is highly encouraged.\n- **Efficiency**: Focus on making code self-explanatory rather than writing redundant comments.\n\n### Testing Guidelines\nWe expect well-tested code that follows these principles:\n\n| Test Type | Primary Focus | Constraints |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Unit** | Atomic behavior and correctness. | No mocking or stubbing. Test behavior, not implementation details. |\n| **Integration** | Component interaction. | Focus on integration and schema, not individual data correctness. |\n| **System** | End-to-end behavior and time-based logic. | Avoid non-functional metrics like performance. |\n\n### What to Deprioritize\n- **Cosmetics**: Consistency is more important than specific stylistic choices.\n- **Performance**: We look for bottlenecks but don't expect micro-optimizations.\n- **Extras**: Logging, monitoring, and extensive documentation are optional \"plus points.\"\n- **Security**: Deep security or risk management can be ignored for this challenge.\n\n## The Interview Process\nAfter submission, we will discuss your solution in an interview focused on:\n1. **Code Walkthrough**: Your architectural choices, trade-offs, and how a junior developer would navigate your code.\n2. **Experience**: Your approach to the challenge, navigation of time constraints, and avoidance of \"copy-paste\" pitfalls.\n3. **Problem Solving**: How you handled any \"pre-designed issues\" within the challenge.\n\n\n[Add project notes](https://github.com/lfb0801/lfb0801/issues/new?template=project-notes.yml&title=Project+notes%3A+JavaPracticeChallenge&project=JavaPracticeChallenge)\n\n[All projects](/projects/README.md)\n"},{"path":"/projects/github/pickleball-hussel-systeem.md","type":"file","markdown":"# pickleball-hussel-systeem\n\n[View on GitHub](https://github.com/lfb0801/pickleball-hussel-systeem)\n\nIndelingssysteem voor pickleball-clubavonden\n\n## README\n\n# Pickleball hussel\n\n[![Tests](https://github.com/lfb0801/pickleball-hussel-systeem/actions/workflows/tests.yml/badge.svg)](https://github.com/lfb0801/pickleball-hussel-systeem/actions/workflows/tests.yml)\n\nA court scheduler for pickleball club evenings. Each round it decides who plays with and against whom on which court, grouped by level, and rotates rest turns fairly. It keeps no scores or rankings.\n\nThe app is available in Dutch (default) and English; the language can be switched at any time from the header, on the laptop and on each phone separately.\n\n**Live:** <https://lfb0801.github.io/pickleball-hussel-systeem/>\n\n## How it works\n\nOne laptop or tablet in the hall runs the evening. Players join with their phone by scanning a QR code, sign in with their name and level, see their court, and can pause or leave on their own.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  P1[Phone] <-- WebRTC --> L[Laptop in the hall]\n  P2[Phone] <-- WebRTC --> L\n  L -. discovery .- S[Public PeerJS server]\n```\n\n- **No server, no database.** The app is a set of static files on GitHub Pages. Phones connect directly to the laptop via [PeerJS](https://peerjs.com/); only finding each other goes through the free public PeerJS server.\n- **The laptop is the single source of truth.** Phones send requests; the laptop applies them and sends the new state back.\n- **Phones are a convenience, not a requirement.** Everything a phone can do, the laptop can do too, even without internet.\n- **Checking in works without internet.** Each phone can make a check-in pass: a QR code with its phone id, name and level. The laptop camera reads it and checks the player in, signing up someone new on the spot. The pass doesn't depend on the session, so it (or a screenshot of it) works every week. A phone that gets a connection later is already linked to its player.\n- **Opened once, works offline.** A service worker (`sw.js`) keeps a copy of both pages, so they open in the hall without internet once they've been opened before. iPhones clear that copy after about a week without a visit, which is why the pass suggests a screenshot.\n- **No build step.** Plain `<script>` tags instead of ES modules, so everything also works from `file://` and the same code can be tested under Node.\n\n## Project structure\n\n```\n├── index.html                 The phone app (base URL, via the QR code)\n├── host/index.html            The laptop app (organiser), at /host/\n├── player.html, speler.html   Redirects for old links to the phone app\n├── sw.js                      Service worker: offline copy of both pages\n├── assets/\n│   ├── css/                   theme.css (club branding), style.css, player.css\n│   ├── js/\n│   │   ├── core/              Logic without screens: pairing algorithm, presets, session rules,\n│   │   │   │                  check-in pass, storage + migration, network, offline, i18n\n│   │   │   └── locales/       nl.js (default) and en.js: every visible text\n│   │   ├── laptop/            App start-up, the laptop screens and the pass scanner\n│   │   └── phone/             The phone app\n│   └── vendor/                PeerJS 1.5.4, qrcode-generator 1.4.4 and jsQR 1.4.0, pinned\n├── tests/\n│   ├── tests.js               Pairing, session and storage tests\n│   ├── migrate-tests.js       Tests for upgrading data saved by older versions\n│   ├── pass-tests.js          Tests for the check-in pass and checking in by scan\n│   ├── index.html             Test runner in the browser\n│   └── node.js                The same tests under Node\n└── scripts/\n    └── stamp-version.js       Appends a ?v= version to every local script and stylesheet\n```\n\n`assets/js/core/pairing.js` (the algorithm) and `assets/js/core/session.js` (applying requests from phones) are pure functions without DOM or network access. That makes every schedule reproducible from its input and testable without a browser.\n\n## Languages\n\nAll visible text lives in `assets/js/core/locales/`. Static text in the HTML refers to it with `data-t=\"key\"` (and `data-t-placeholder`, `data-t-title`, `data-t-aria-label`); text with values filled in is a function. To add a language, add a locale file with the same keys, load it in both HTML pages and add its code to `I18n.LANGUAGES` in `assets/js/core/i18n.js`. Keys missing from a locale fall back to Dutch.\n\n## Running locally\n\nNothing to install. Open `host/index.html` directly in a browser (without the offline copy; the camera may not work from a local file), or start a local server:\n\n```bash\nnpm start\n```\n\nand go to <http://127.0.0.1:8080/>. Phones on the same network need a server that listens on the network address. On localhost the service worker fetches from the network first, so changes show up straight away.\n\n## Tests\n\n```bash\nnpm test\n```\n\nRun a subset by filtering on the test name: `node tests/node.js session`. The same tests run in the browser at `tests/index.html`.\n\n## Releasing\n\nThe app runs on GitHub Pages from the `master` branch. Pages lets browsers cache files for ten minutes, so before every commit you push, stamp a new version on all local files so a browser never mixes old and new scripts:\n\n```bash\nnpm run stamp\n```\n\nThis also bumps the version in `sw.js`. Browsers then fetch the new release for their offline copy in the background and use it from the next time the page opens.\n\n\n[Add project notes](https://github.com/lfb0801/lfb0801/issues/new?template=project-notes.yml&title=Project+notes%3A+pickleball-hussel-systeem&project=pickleball-hussel-systeem)\n\n[All projects](/projects/README.md)\n"}];
